  "question": {
    "text": "The value of ' $a$ ' for which one root of the quadratic equation $\\left(a^2-5 a+3\\right) x^3+(3 a-1) x+2=0$ is twice as large as the other is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "XWNnQ",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{1}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "lYyzY",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{2}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "iYpdV",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{2}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "EJP6v",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$3 \\alpha=\\frac{1-3 a}{a^2-5 a+3} \\& 2 \\alpha^2=\\frac{2}{a^2-5 a+3}$\n$2\\left[\\frac{1}{9} \\frac{(1-3 a)^2}{\\left(a^2-5 a+3\\right)^2}\\right]=\\frac{2}{a^2-5 a+3}$\n$\\frac{(1-3 a)^2}{\\left(a^2-5 a+3\\right)}=9$ or $9 a^2-6 a+1$\n$=9 a^2-45 a+27$ or $39 a=26$ or $a=\\frac{2}{3}$",
    "image": null
  },
